<h1>Vertical Reciprocating Conveyors (VRCs)</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A vertical reciprocating conveyor (VRC) lifts materials between two or more levels on a guided carriage. VRCs are classified as conveyors under <a href="https://www.asme.org/codes-standards/find-codes-standards/b20-1-safety-standard-conveyors-related-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASME B20.1</a>, not elevators, so they are not built to the elevator code. Published capacities run from light package lifts to heavy four-post units rated in the tens of thousands of pounds. VRCs are the standard way to move pallets, carts, and totes between a floor and a mezzanine in packaging, distribution, and manufacturing plants. No one may ride a VRC.</p>
<h2 id="types-heading">Drive Types and Mast Configurations</h2>
<p>A VRC is specified on two separate axes: how it lifts (mechanical or hydraulic drive) and how the carriage is supported (mast configuration). The <a href="https://www.pflow.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/MHI_CSS_vrc_application_guidelines_pdf.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MHI Application Guidelines for Vertical Reciprocating Conveyors</a>, written by the VRC subcommittee of MHI's Conveyor and Sortation Systems product group, defines four mast configurations.</p>
<h3>Mechanical (chain or cable) VRC</h3>
<p>A brake motor and gear reducer drive roller chains or cables that raise and lower the carriage. Mechanical units cover the widest range of capacity, travel height, and number of stops. <a href="https://autoquip.com/blog/mechanical-vs-hydraulic-vertical-reciprocating-conveyers-vrcs-what-is-best-for-my-application/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Autoquip</a> rates its mechanical VRCs up to 20,000 lbs and up to 100 feet of travel. PFlow rates its two-post <a href="https://www.pflow.com/vertical-conveyors/mechanical/m-series/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">M Series</a> up to 10,000 lbs at a standard 25 FPM, with higher speeds available for high-cycle automated systems.</p>
<h3>Hydraulic VRC</h3>
<p>An electric motor drives a hydraulic pump that extends cylinders to raise the carriage. Hydraulic units suit lower travel heights and two-level service. Autoquip rates its hydraulic VRCs up to 6,000 lbs with less than 30 feet of travel. PFlow rates its <a href="https://www.pflow.com/vertical-conveyors/hydraulic/21-series/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">21 Series</a> hydraulic lift up to 6,000 lbs, 22 feet of rise, two levels, and 10 cycles per hour at the standard 24 FPM. Autoquip describes hydraulic units as the less expensive option where their height and capacity limits fit.</p>
