<h2 id="safety-heading">VRC Safety Standards and Code Requirements</h2>
<h3>ASME B20.1</h3>
<p>ASME B20.1 is the safety standard for VRCs. The MHI guidelines note that in 2015 B20.1 added a mandatory appendix covering VRC design, installation, commissioning, and periodic inspection. ASME A17.1, the elevator code, expressly excludes B20 conveyors, including VRCs, from its scope (A17.1 Section 1.1.2, as cited by MHI). Some elevator inspectors still treat VRCs as A17.1 Type A material lifts. The MHI guidelines were written in part to address that confusion.</p>
<h3>OSHA</h3>
<p>MHI states that VRCs are typically regulated and inspected by OSHA. Maintenance work must follow <a href="https://www.osha.gov/laws-regs/regulations/standardnumber/1910/1910.147" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OSHA 1910.147 lockout/tagout</a>, and the carriage must be mechanically blocked per the manufacturer's procedure before anyone works under it.</p>
<h3>Building code</h3>
<p>According to MHI, many state and local jurisdictions have adopted the International Building Code (IBC), which recognizes VRCs under ASME B20.1 by reference in Chapter 30. Local building officials may still require special permitting or testing, and the owner is responsible for confirming that. Gates on a VRC do not replace fire doors where the floor opening requires them. Confirm with your authority having jurisdiction (AHJ).</p>
<h3>Key safety features</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Enclosures:</strong> B20.1 requires 96-inch-high enclosures at each floor level. At the top landing, 42-inch handrails may be permitted as part of the guarding in some cases. Mesh openings must reject a 2-inch ball, and enclosures must withstand 200 lbs of force in any direction (MHI Section 10.1).</li>
<li><strong>Interlocked gates:</strong> The VRC cannot run while any gate is open, and a gate cannot open unless the carriage is at that landing. MHI notes that VRCs use a mechanical lock plus an electric gate status switch, not an A17-type elevator door interlock.</li>
<li><strong>Limit switches:</strong> Upper and lower travel limit switches, plus overtravel sensors on mechanical units.</li>
<li><strong>Slack chain or cable detection:</strong> On mechanical units, sensors stop the lift if a chain or cable goes slack. Many units also have safety cams or carriage brakes that arrest the carriage if a chain fails.</li>
<li><strong>Velocity fuse (hydraulic):</strong> A hydraulic valve that locks oil in the cylinder if a hose is severed, so the carriage cannot descend.</li>
<li><strong>Controls off the carriage:</strong> VRC operator controls are never reachable from the carriage (MHI).</li>
<li><strong>Emergency stops:</strong> E-stop buttons at each operating station.</li>
</ul>
<h3>VRCs cannot carry people</h3>
<p>VRCs are material-only conveyors. No one may ride the carriage, and signage at each landing must say so. If people must ride with the load, the application needs an elevator built to ASME A17.1.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">VRC Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Mezzanine material transport:</strong> The most common VRC application. VRCs lift pallets, cases, and totes between the ground floor and mezzanine pick modules in distribution centers and warehouses. <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyors</a> feed pallets onto the VRC platform at the lower level and receive them at the upper level.</p>
<p><strong>Multi-level <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging facilities</a>:</strong> Packaging plants with production on one floor and warehousing on another use VRCs to move cased goods and pallets between levels. The VRC integrates with horizontal <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor</a> systems via <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer conveyors</a> at each level.</p>
<p><strong><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food and beverage:</a></strong> VRCs move product between processing floors and packaging areas. Galvanized, epoxy, or <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary stainless</a> finishes are available for washdown areas.</p>
<p><strong>Manufacturing:</strong> VRCs move raw materials from receiving docks to elevated production floors and return finished goods to ground-level shipping. Four-post mechanical units handle the heaviest loads, such as large fabricated assemblies.</p>
<p><strong>Retail distribution:</strong> Multi-level retail distribution centers use VRCs to move merchandise between receiving, pick floors, and shipping levels. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyor</a> systems on each level connect to VRCs via powered <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation zones</a> that stage product for lift cycles.</p>
<p><strong>When to choose a VRC over a spiral:</strong> Choose a VRC when loads are heavy or large (pallets, carts), when the required rate fits a batch cycle, or when the lift height is beyond what a spiral can practically cover. Choose a <a href="/conveyor-types/spiral-conveyors/">spiral conveyor</a> for continuous flow of cases and totes. The VRC lifts one load per cycle, while a spiral moves product without interruption.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">How to Specify a VRC</h2>
<p>VRC specification requires defining these key parameters (MHI Section 5 covers each in more detail):</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Load weight and size:</strong> Maximum load weight, carriage dimensions (width x depth x height clearance), and whether the load is a pallet, cart, tote, or mixed.</li>
<li><strong>Load orientation and loading pattern:</strong> Which sides you load and unload from. Same side, opposite ends ("Z"), or adjacent sides determines straddle, cantilever, or four-post.</li>
<li><strong>Travel height:</strong> Floor-to-floor distance between the lowest and highest stop. Include any pit or overhead clearance constraints.</li>
<li><strong>Number of stops:</strong> Two-stop (ground plus mezzanine) is the most common. Hydraulic units are usually limited to two levels.</li>
<li><strong>Throughput requirement:</strong> Loads per hour required. Calculate cycle time: travel time (up + down) + load time + unload time + gate open/close time. Divide 60 minutes by cycle time to get max loads per hour. Confirm the rated duty cycle with the manufacturer.</li>
<li><strong>Loading method:</strong> Fork truck, hand pallet jack, powered <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">conveyor</a>, or manual cart push. Conveyor-fed VRCs integrate with horizontal <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR</a> or <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">powered roller</a> lines for automated loading and unloading.</li>
<li><strong>Gates and enclosures:</strong> Gate type at each level and who supplies the enclosure. The installer must make sure the full installation meets B20.1 regardless of who supplies the gates.</li>
<li><strong>Environment:</strong> Indoor or outdoor, temperature range, washdown, and hazardous area classification affect finish and component ratings.</li>
<li><strong>Code jurisdiction:</strong> Confirm local AHJ requirements. Some jurisdictions require permits, inspections, or testing beyond ASME B20.1.</li>
</ul>
<p>See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for VRC cost factors. <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">System integrators</a> can design the complete horizontal and vertical conveyor system as a turnkey project.</p>
<h2 id="evaluate-heading">How to Evaluate VRC Options</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Standard compliance:</strong> Ask the manufacturer to confirm in writing that the unit is designed and built to ASME B20.1, including the VRC appendix.</li>
<li><strong>Rated capacity at your carriage size:</strong> Capacity often depends on carriage size and options. Get the rating for your exact carriage, not the series maximum.</li>
<li><strong>Duty cycle:</strong> Compare rated cycles per hour and per day against your calculated requirement.</li>
<li><strong>Fall arrest:</strong> Ask how the carriage is held if a chain, cable, or hose fails (safety cams, carriage brakes, velocity fuse).</li>
<li><strong>Gate and enclosure scope:</strong> Clarify who supplies gates and enclosures and who signs off that the installation meets B20.1.</li>
<li><strong>Installation and commissioning:</strong> Ask for the startup test procedure and the periodic inspection schedule.</li>
</ul>
<p>Get proposals from at least two VRC manufacturers on the same specification.</p>
