<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a vertical reciprocating conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A VRC is a hydraulic or mechanical lift that moves materials between levels on a guided carriage. VRCs are classified as conveyors under ASME B20.1, not elevators, and ASME A17.1 excludes B20 conveyors from its scope. No one may ride the carriage. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/">complete conveyor type guide</a> for other vertical transport options.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">VRC vs freight elevator, what is the difference?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>VRCs move material only and are built to ASME B20.1. Freight elevators can carry people and are built to ASME A17.1. Per MHI's VRC application guidelines, VRCs normally run below 100 FPM, ride on rollers in guide columns, never have controls on the carriage, and are typically regulated by OSHA. Elevators run on T-rails in a hoistway and are typically inspected by a state or municipal authority. Some jurisdictions still require permits or testing for a VRC.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is the weight capacity of a VRC?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>It depends on drive type, mast configuration, and carriage size. Autoquip rates its mechanical VRCs up to 20,000 lbs and hydraulic VRCs up to 6,000 lbs. PFlow rates its two-post M Series up to 10,000 lbs and its four-post F Series up to 50,000 lbs standard, customizable to 200,000 lbs. Four-post designs carry the highest loads.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What safety standards apply to VRCs?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>ASME B20.1 governs VRCs, and since 2015 it has included a mandatory VRC appendix covering design, installation, commissioning, and periodic inspection. Key requirements: 96-inch enclosures at each level (handrails may form part of the guarding at the top landing in some cases), interlocked gates, travel limit switches, and slack chain or cable sensors on mechanical units. OSHA lockout/tagout applies during maintenance. IBC Chapter 30 recognizes VRCs under B20.1, and local officials may require permits or testing.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">How much does a VRC cost compared to an elevator?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A VRC is generally the lower-cost option because it is built to the conveyor standard instead of the elevator code. VRC cost drivers are capacity, travel height, number of levels, carriage size, drive type, gates and enclosures, and any local permitting or testing. A freight elevator adds a hoistway, elevator-grade rails and door interlocks, and elevator inspection. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for cost factors.</p>

</div>
</div>
